vm-1 — Bytecode ISA + compiled-function object layout (plan-2-bytecode-vm).

Design (no execution yet) the instruction set and the runtime object.

WHAT
- A STACK VM (simplest to compile to and port).  Opcode = 1 byte + inline operands
  (varint indices / signed jump offsets).  Draft set:
    stack:   PUSH-CONST i, PUSH-NIL, PUSH-T, DUP, POP, DROP n
    locals:  LOAD-LOCAL depth idx, STORE-LOCAL depth idx  (lexical addresses)
    globals: LOAD-GLOBAL sym-i, STORE-GLOBAL sym-i, LOAD-FUNCTION sym-i
    control: JUMP off, JUMP-IF-NIL off, JUMP-IF-NOT-NIL off
    calls:   CALL n, CALL-NAMED sym-i n, TAIL-CALL n, RETURN
    values:  VALUES n, MV-CALL, MV-BIND ...
    nlx:     make-block/return-from, catch/throw, unwind-protect enter/leave
             (design the frame markers; details in vm-5)
    closure: MAKE-CLOSURE tmpl-i, LOAD-UPVAL i (design in vm-6)
- Compiled-function object (extend the kernel object zoo): { name/lisp_name,
  lambda_list (for FLE/arglist), code: octet-vector, constants: simple-vector,
  n-args / &optional / &rest / &key descriptor, max-stack, n-locals, source (opt) }.
  It must be a FUNCTION subtype so FUNCTIONP / the APPLY path accept it alongside
  the tree-walked FUNCTION and BUILT-IN-FUNCTION (mind the built_in_function_p-first
  ordering, see the %FUNCTION-LAMBDA-BODY note).
- Value representation: reuse the existing tagged Ref (no new NaN-boxing) so the VM
  and the tree-walker share values with zero conversion.
- FLE / DISASSEMBLE: keep the source lambda expression (or a decompiler) so
  FUNCTION-LAMBDA-EXPRESSION still returns something useful for a bytecode function.

ACCEPTANCE: a documented ISA + object layout that vm-2 (VM) and vm-4/5 (codegen) can
target; agreed opcode list and operand encodings; a written stack-effect table.

DEPENDS ON: none (first of plan-2).  Reuse decisions from [[bocl-defun-lexical-block]]
(lexical blocks already live in bodies -> BLOCK is a normal compilable special op).
